    <h1>Due Date Explained</h1>

    <p>Your due date is one of the most anticipated numbers in pregnancy. But what exactly is it, how is it calculated, and how accurate is it? This guide explains everything you need to know.</p>

    <h2>What Is a Due Date?</h2>

    <p>Your due date (estimated date of delivery, or EDD) is an estimate of when your baby will be born. It is calculated as 40 weeks from the first day of your last menstrual period (LMP).</p>

    <p>The due date is not a deadline. It is a midpoint in a range of normal. Most babies are born between 37 and 42 weeks.</p>

    <p>Your midwife usually works out the date at your first appointment, which is offered at 8 to 12 weeks. If you do not know the first day of your last period, or you are unsure, the date is worked out from a scan instead.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS due date calculator, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/finding-out/due-date-calculator/; NHS antenatal care and appointments, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/your-pregnancy-care/your-antenatal-care-and-appointments/; ACOG Committee Opinion 700, https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2017/05/methods-for-estimating-the-due-date</em></p>

    <h2>How Is the Due Date Calculated?</h2>

    <p>Clinicians have several methods to choose from. The method used depends on what is known about your cycle, the pregnancy, and any ultrasound measurements.</p>

    <h3>Naegele's Rule (LMP Method)</h3>
    <p><strong>EDD = LMP + 280 days</strong></p>
    <p>This is the standard method. It assumes a 28-day cycle with ovulation on day 14.</p>

    <p>The rule has been used for about 200 years. It counts 280 days, or 40 weeks, forward from the first day of your last period. That is a convention rather than a measured date.</p>

    <p>The calculation assumes a regular 28-day cycle with ovulation on day 14. It does not account for poor recall of the last period, irregular cycle length, or variation in the timing of ovulation. ACOG reports that about one half of women recall their LMP accurately.</p>

    <p>In one study, 40% of women who had a first-trimester ultrasound had their due date adjusted because the ultrasound and the period-based date differed by more than 5 days. In a comparison group who had an ultrasound in the second trimester, the date was adjusted in only 10% of women.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: ACOG Committee Opinion 700, https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2017/05/methods-for-estimating-the-due-date; Royal Berkshire NHS Foundation Trust, https://www.royalberkshire.nhs.uk/media/r0fcsaml/baby-due-dates-and-how-it-affects-my-care.pdf</em></p>

    <AdUnit slot="925728676" format="auto" />
    <h3>Cycle-Corrected LMP</h3>
    <p><strong>EDD = LMP + 280 + (cycle length − 28)</strong></p>
    <p>This adjusts for cycles that are not exactly 28 days. For example, a 32-day cycle adds 4 days to the due date.</p>

    <p>The correction matters most if your cycles are longer than 28 days or vary from month to month. If your cycles are irregular, or you cannot remember your last period, ACOG recommends dating the pregnancy from ultrasound measurements, ideally taken before or at 13 6/7 weeks.</p>

    <p><em>Source: ACOG Committee Opinion 700, https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2017/05/methods-for-estimating-the-due-date</em></p>

    <h2>Conception and IVF Dating</h2>

    <h3>Conception Date</h3>
    <p><strong>EDD = Conception date + 266 days</strong></p>
    <p>This is more accurate if you know the conception date (for example, from IVF or known intercourse).</p>

    <p>Counting 266 days from conception gives the same date as counting 280 days from the first day of your last period, on the assumption that conception happened on day 14. Knowing the conception date removes that assumption.</p>

    <p><em>Source: ACOG Committee Opinion 700, https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2017/05/methods-for-estimating-the-due-date</em></p>

    <h3>IVF Transfer</h3>
    <p><strong>EDD = Transfer date + 263 days (Day 3) or + 261 days (Day 5)</strong></p>
    <p>IVF dating is the most precise method because the exact date of fertilization is known.</p>

    <p>ACOG recommends using the IVF-derived date and not re-dating an IVF pregnancy from a scan. For a day-5 embryo the due date is 261 days from the embryo replacement date. For a day-3 embryo it is 263 days from the replacement date.</p>

    <p><em>Source: ACOG Committee Opinion 700, https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2017/05/methods-for-estimating-the-due-date</em></p>

    <h2>Ultrasound Dating</h2>
    <p><strong>EDD = Ultrasound date + (280 − gestational age at ultrasound in days)</strong></p>

    <p>First-trimester ultrasound is the most accurate dating method. Measuring the crown-rump length up to and including 13 6/7 weeks gives an accuracy of plus or minus 5 to 7 days, and it is more accurate the earlier in the first trimester it is performed.</p>

    <p>ACOG sets cutoffs for changing the due date when an ultrasound disagrees with the period-based date. Change the date if the difference is more than 7 days before 14 0/7 weeks, more than 5 days before 9 0/7 weeks, more than 7 days at 14 0/7 to 15 6/7 weeks, more than 10 days at 16 0/7 to 21 6/7 weeks, more than 14 days at 22 0/7 to 27 6/7 weeks, or more than 21 days in the third trimester.</p>

    <p>Dating becomes less precise as pregnancy progresses. Second-trimester ultrasound has an accuracy of plus or minus 7 to 10 days between 14 0/7 and 21 6/7 weeks, and plus or minus 10 to 14 days between 22 0/7 and 27 6/7 weeks. Third-trimester dating is the least reliable, at plus or minus 21 to 30 days, so the date is moved then only if the discrepancy is more than 21 days.</p>

    <p>A pregnancy with no ultrasound that confirms or revises the due date before 22 0/7 weeks is considered suboptimally dated.</p>

    <p><em>Source: ACOG Committee Opinion 700, https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2017/05/methods-for-estimating-the-due-date</em></p>

    <h2>How Accurate Is the Due Date?</h2>

    <p>Only about <strong>5% of babies are born on their exact due date</strong>. NHS guidance describes this as about 1 in 20. Most are born before or after the date.</p>

    <p>The normal range of birth is 37 to 42 weeks from the first day of your last period, and most babies are born by 41 weeks.</p>

    <p>The due date is most accurate when:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your cycles are regular</li>
      <li>You know the date of your last period</li>
      <li>A first-trimester ultrasound confirms the LMP-based date</li>
    </ul>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS hospital bag checklist, https://www.nhs.uk/best-start-in-life/pregnancy/preparing-for-labour-and-birth/hospital-bag-checklist/; NHS overdue, https://www.nhs.uk/best-start-in-life/pregnancy/preparing-for-labour-and-birth/overdue-have-you-gone-past-your-due-date/; Royal Berkshire NHS Foundation Trust, https://www.royalberkshire.nhs.uk/media/r0fcsaml/baby-due-dates-and-how-it-affects-my-care.pdf</em></p>

    <AdUnit slot="2263226824" format="auto" />
    <h2>What If the Due Date Changes?</h2>

    <p>It is not uncommon for the due date to change during pregnancy, usually at the dating scan. A change is more likely when:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You cannot recall the first day of your last period, or you are unsure of it</li>
      <li>Your cycles are irregular, so the period-based date is unreliable</li>
      <li>An early ultrasound disagrees with the period-based date by more than the cutoff for that stage of pregnancy</li>
    </ul>

    <p>If your due date changes, your clinician will explain why. A change of a few days is normal.</p>

    <p><em>Source: ACOG Committee Opinion 700, https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2017/05/methods-for-estimating-the-due-date</em></p>

