    <h2>What Happens After the Due Date?</h2>

    <p>Going a few days past your due date is common. NHS advice is that it is usually safe to go up to a week past your due date, from 40 to 41 weeks.</p>

    <p>Once you reach 41 weeks the risks start to increase, although they remain very small. They include a caesarean birth, your baby needing care in a neonatal unit, stillbirth, and neonatal death.</p>

    <p>Induction is offered if you have not gone into labour naturally by 41 weeks, because the risk of stillbirth or problems for the baby is then higher. Before induction, a membrane sweep is usually offered after 39 weeks.</p>

    <p>If your pregnancy lasts longer than 42 weeks and you decide not to have your labour induced, you should be offered increased monitoring to check your baby's wellbeing. Around 1 in 5 births are induced.</p>

    <p>There are no proven ways to bring on labour safely at home. If you are unsure or worried about anything, contact your midwife or maternity unit.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS inducing labour, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/labour-and-birth/inducing-labour/; NHS 41 weeks pregnant, https://www.nhs.uk/best-start-in-life/pregnancy/week-by-week-guide-to-pregnancy/3rd-trimester/week-41/; NHS overdue, https://www.nhs.uk/best-start-in-life/pregnancy/preparing-for-labour-and-birth/overdue-have-you-gone-past-your-due-date/; NHS antenatal care and appointments, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/your-pregnancy-care/your-antenatal-care-and-appointments/</em></p>

    <h2>When to Call Your Clinician</h2>

    <p>Contact your healthcare provider if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You are unsure of your due date</li>
      <li>Your due date has changed</li>
      <li>You are past your due date</li>
      <li>You have signs of labor before 37 weeks</li>
    </ul>

